  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nWraparound moisture/clouds from the lingering nor'easter will\ncontinue to spin across our region today in northerly flow. A\nfew sprinkles or light rain showers remain possible near and\nnorth of Pittsburgh this afternoon, especially in upslope-prone\nregions, but limited lift and instability will keep any \nprecipitation totals very light. Temperatures should continue to\nrun a few degrees below climatology, save for areas near ZZV\nthat are receiving more sunshine and will be a bit closer to\nnormal.\n\nThe upper low associated with the coastal system will drift\nnorthward across southern New England tonight towards the Gulf\nof Maine, finally devolving into an open wave over the Canadian\nMaritimes tomorrow night. Clouds will begin to break tonight as\nthis process begins. Any locations that manages to clear may\nquickly fog in given the nearly saturated boundary layer and a\nvery weak surface pressure gradient.\n\nThe cloud decrease continues Tuesday/Tuesday night as mid-level\nridging crosses the Upper Ohio Valley. Surface high pressure\nalso arrives and remains in control into Thursday. The increased\nsunshine and rising heights will push temperatures back above\nnormal while maintaining dry conditions. NBM probabilities for \n80F or greater high temperatures that day remain in the 55 to \n80 percent range for most non-ridge locations south of I-80.\nDewpoints in the lower 60s that day, however, point to less heat\nstress - probabilities for Moderate or higher HeatRisk values\nremain under 20 percent for the most part.\n\n500mb flow flattens after Thursday as a shortwave trough crosses\neastern Canada. Strength and timing issues remain with this\ndisturbance, although most of the uncertainty lies to our north.\nThat said, at least scattered shower activity becomes more \nlikely Thursday night/Friday in the moistening warm sector with\nan approaching front, with rain chances lingering into the \nweekend. Severe weather potential looks muted by meager \ninstability, and probabilities for problematic rainfall totals \nremain quite low.",
